---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import Contact from '../components/home/Contact.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import { coverage } from '../data/coverage';
const education = (await getCollection('education')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — Mira Purkrábek"
  description="Miroslav Purkrábek: computer vision research, engineering, teaching at CTU Prague, floorball coaching, and life outside work."
>
  <Section size="text">
    <h1>About me</h1>
    <Prose>
      <p class="lead">
        I'm Mira. I work in computer vision, used to spend most evenings in a
        floorball hall, and like being in the mountains whenever I can.
      </p>
      <h2>Research, via a sports hall</h2>
      <p>
        My first computer vision projects grew out of floorball. For my
        bachelor's and master's theses at CTU, I worked on tracking players
        across cameras. It was a way to connect something I was studying with a
        sport I already spent a lot of time thinking about.
      </p>
      <p>
        At Porsche Engineering, I worked on Macan control-unit software and on
        the High Power Charging platform for Taycan and other electric vehicles.
        I've been a researcher in the <a href="https://vrg.fel.cvut.cz/"
          >Visual Recognition Group at CTU Prague</a
        > since February 2019, and started my PhD in February 2023 with{' '}
        <a href="https://cmp.felk.cvut.cz/~matas/">Jiří Matas</a>. Since then I've visited <a href="https://virtualhumans.mpi-inf.mpg.de/"
          >Gerard Pons-Moll's group in Tübingen</a
        > (December 2025–March 2026) and joined Qualcomm XR Labs in Amsterdam
        for a research internship (June–December 2026).
      </p>
      <p>
        Human pose estimation was my starting point: understanding how people
        move and how to recover that reliably from images. My PhD grew from
        there into broader human body analysis, including detection, segmentation,
        pose and shape. I'm now extending that work into 3D reconstruction,
        spatial reasoning and how vision-language models understand people in
        their surroundings. At Qualcomm, I explore these questions in the
        context of personal AI assistants.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'm drawn to the gap between a promising research result and something
        people can rely on. I think of it as the “last 10%”: the difficult cases
        and practical constraints that a strong benchmark score can hide.
        When I hear that a problem is solved, my first instinct is to stress-test
        that claim. What happens with an unfamiliar camera angle, a crowded
        scene or a body partly outside the frame?
      </p>
      <p>
        For me, those failures are often where the next research question begins.
        I like developing new methods with a concrete use in mind, letting the
        needs of the people using a system shape what I investigate and how I
        evaluate it. The aim is to advance what works in practice as well as
        what is possible in research.
      </p>
      <p>
        That interest has led to <a href="/publications/"
          >ProbPose, BBox-Mask-Pose and RePoGen</a
        >, as well as datasets and evaluation metrics including CropCOCO,
        Extended OKS and OCHuman-Pose. I release code, models and demos, and
        build tools with the people using them: police analysts,
        infant-development researchers and floorball coaches. <a href="/work/"
          >All projects</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/publications/#recognition">Awards, talks and service</a> · <a
          href={site.cvPath}>Two-page CV</a
        > · <a href="/news/">Earlier notes, 2022–2026</a>
      </p>
      <h2 id="teaching">Teaching and supervision</h2>
      <p>
        I teach <a href="https://cw.fel.cvut.cz/wiki/courses/b4b33rpz/start"
          >RPZ — Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning</a
        > at CTU. The course covers statistical decision theory, perceptrons, AdaBoost,
        support vector machines and neural networks.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also co-supervise research, including <a
          href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08982">SAM-pose2seg</a
        >, awarded Best Student Paper at CVWW 2026, and <a
          href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.08815">PC-CSE</a
        >. Students I supervise have won prizes before even finishing their
        bachelor's degrees. Helping someone find their own research direction
        and write their first paper is a part of the work I particularly enjoy.
        I also coordinate our team's annotation work. I'm happy to discuss thesis
        ideas and research opportunities in my field.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>For homework questions, please use the student forum.</li><li>
          I don't keep fixed consultation hours; <a
            href={`mailto:${site.email.university}`}>email me</a
          > to arrange a meeting.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="coaching">A few years on the sidelines</h2>
      <p>
        I joined Sokol Královské Vinohrady in 2017 as an assistant coach. During
        Erasmus, I also coached FBK Olimpija in Ljubljana for a few months. Back
        in Prague, I took over as juniors' head coach, then became head coach of
        the men's Superliga team in 2024. We worked with a young team and a
        small budget, and reached the best results in the club's history through
        clear roles, shared effort and team spirit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those years taught me to enjoy both sides of a team: supporting someone
        else's direction as an assistant, and taking responsibility as head
        coach. I bring that same approach to research: contribute to the shared
        work, help others move forward, and lead when needed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some of the juniors I coached now play for the senior team. Watching
        that progression is what I remember most fondly. I stepped down in
        summer 2025 to focus on research, and stayed on as a consultant.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/coaching/">More about coaching</a>, including the seasons, the
        people, and a podcast conversation in Czech.
      </p>
      <h2 id="personal">Away from work</h2>
      <p>
        I enjoy mountains, travel, speciality coffee, psychology and epic
        fantasy. Steven Erikson’s <em>Malazan Book of the Fallen</em> is a
        particular favourite. The coffee hobby once became a small{' '}
        <a href="/work/#vrg-coffee">coffee-rating web app for our group at VRG</a>.
      </p>
      <h2 id="education">Education</h2>
      <ul class="education-list">
        {education.map(({ data }) => (
          <li>
            <h3>{data.degree} · {data.field}</h3>
            <p class="detail-meta">{data.period} · {data.institution}</p>
            {data.note && <p>{data.note}</p>}
            {data.thesisLink && <a href={data.thesisLink}>Thesis</a>}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>
        During my Erasmus exchange (October 2018–March 2019), I had my first
        computer vision research internship at the University of Ljubljana's{' '}
        <a href="https://www.vicos.si/">Visual Cognitive Systems Laboratory</a>,
        advised by prof. Matej Kristan.
      </p>
      <h2 id="skills">Technical skills</h2>
      <p>
        These are my core skills. AI-assisted coding (or “vibe coding”) now helps
        me pick up other technologies quickly and put them to practical use.
      </p>
      <dl class="skills">
        <dt>Python</dt>
        <dd>Research implementations and deep learning, including training PMPose-h
        for more than ten days on eight GPUs using SLURM; daily automated testing
        at Porsche.</dd>
        <dt>C / C++</dt>
        <dd>Production automotive control-unit software, Plug &amp; Charge for
        Porsche's charger, and an autonomous robot in a university course.</dd>
        <dt>Frameworks &amp; tools</dt>
        <dd>PyTorch, OpenCV, SLURM, Git, SMPL, CUDA, Blender and MMCV.</dd>
      </dl>
      <h2 id="elsewhere">Elsewhere</h2>
      <p class="detail-meta">Interviews, features &amp; coverage</p>
      <ul class="coverage-list">
        {coverage.map((item) => (
          <li>
            <a href={item.url}>{item.title}</a>
            <span class="detail-meta">{item.source} · {item.language}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>
        For a compact overview, <a href={site.cvPath}>download my two-page CV</a>.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </Section>
  <Contact />
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  h1 {
    font-size: var(--text-3xl);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .education-list, .coverage-list { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
  .education-list li, .coverage-list li {
    padding-block: var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .education-list h3 { font-size: var(--text-base); margin-top: 0; }
  .education-list p { margin-top: var(--space-2); color: var(--text-muted); }
  .detail-meta { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--text-subtle); }
  .coverage-list .detail-meta { display: block; margin-top: var(--space-1); }
  .skills dt { font-weight: 600; margin-top: var(--space-4); }
  .skills dd { margin: var(--space-1) 0 0; color: var(--text-muted); }
  .lead {
    font-size: 1.1875rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
